{"title": "Trying to figure out how to build a first emergency fund - any wisdom?", "body": "Hello everyone, I'm trying to get a handle on saving things properly, and I've come across the idea of an emergency fund. I'm retired now, so my expenses aren't as high as they were when I was teaching, but I still want to feel secure about things, you know? 

I'm starting from scratch. I'm not sure where to even begin. Should I aim for three months of living expenses? Six months? I'm worried about setting aside too much money now when I don't have a lot of extra cash lying around.

I've heard different things—some say you need a big cushion immediately, others say you can start small. Has anyone gone through this process? What was your starting point, and how did you manage to save it? I'm open to any practical advice you can offer. Thanks in advance for sharing your experiences."}

{"title": "Anyone else feeling ripped off by dealer markups and add-ons?", "body": "Hey guys, been driving for a while now and trying to figure out the best way to buy a used vehicle, especially with the current market. I just went through a divorce recently, and honestly, it's been a bit of a rough patch, and now I'm looking to make some changes. 

I've been looking at a few options, but I'm getting really frustrated with the way dealerships handle pricing. It feels like they throw in these mandatory 'add-ons'—extended warranties, paint protection, nitrogen in the tires, whatever—and the sticker price is astronomical. I'm trying to figure out if I should just buy something reliable and keep the costs down, or if there's a smart way to navigate this mess. 

Has anyone dealt with this recently? Are there specific dealers or methods you've found that allow you to negotiate those markups down, or are there specific add-ons you absolutely *have* to avoid? Any wisdom on how to avoid getting fleeced? Thanks in advance for any advice."}

